    <string-array name="clinical_eye_tips">
        <item>Incorporate dark leafy greens rich in lutein and zeaxanthin to protect macular pigment density.</item>
        <item>Practice the 20-20-20 rule: Every 20 minutes, look at an object 20 feet away for at least 20 seconds.</item>
        <item>Keep digital screens at eye level or slightly below (15-20 degrees) to decrease tear film evaporation.</item>
        <item>Ensure high-contrast ambient lighting to avoid pupillary strain while reading or viewing displays.</item>
        <item>Always wear UV400-certified protective eyewear during daylight hours to guard against cataracts.</item>
    </string-array>

    <string-array name="clinical_eye_tips_detailed">
        <item>Incorporate dark leafy greens rich in lutein and zeaxanthin (such as spinach, kale, and broccoli) into your daily diet. These carotenoids filter high-energy blue wavelengths and protect retinal pigment epithelium cells against oxidative stress.</item>
        <item>Practice the 20-20-20 rule during digital screen use: Every 20 minutes, shift your gaze to an object at least 20 feet away for at least 20 seconds. This relaxes ciliary muscle accommodation and reduces digital visual fatigue.</item>
        <item>Position digital displays 15 to 20 degrees below eye level from the center of the screen. This positioning decreases interpalpebral fissure width and minimizes corneal tear film evaporation.</item>
        <item>Ensure high-contrast ambient illumination without direct screen reflections or harsh glare. Proper room lighting prevents excessive pupil constriction and decreases asthenopic symptoms during prolonged reading.</item>
        <item>Always wear UV400-certified protective eyewear during daylight hours. Cumulative UV-A and UV-B exposure significantly accelerates nuclear cataract formation and pterygium growth.</item>
    </string-array>

